Statements:
[
    "The input question compares two geological age systems: 'universe, generation, era, and eon' and 'universe, boundary, system, and series.'",
    "A time scale is used to divide the history of the Earth and the universe into various units based on significant events or changes.",
    "The 'universe, generation, era, and eon' system is a hierarchical classification of geological time.",
    "Eon is the largest unit of time, divided into eras.",
    "The current eon is the Phanerozoic, which began 541 million years ago.",
    "Era is a subdivision of an eon, consisting of multiple periods.",
    "The Phanerozoic eon is divided into three eras: Paleozoic, Mesozoic, and Cenozoic.",
    "Generation is an informal term used to describe a subdivision of an era, but it is not officially recognized in the geological time scale.",
    "The 'universe, boundary, system, and series' system is a hierarchical classification of geological time.",
    "Universe is the largest unit of time, equivalent to an eon.",
    "Boundary is a subdivision of a universe, equivalent to an era.",
    "System is a subdivision of a boundary, equivalent to a period.",
    "Series is a subdivision of a system, equivalent to an epoch.",
    "The essential difference between the two systems is the terminology used to describe the various units of time.",
    "Both systems are hierarchical and include similar units.",
    "The 'universe, boundary, system, and series' system uses more formal terminology that is widely accepted in the geological community.",
    "The Mesozoic era is an example of how these two systems can describe the same time period.",
    "In the 'universe, generation, era, and eon' system, the Mesozoic is a generation within the Phanerozoic eon.",
    "In the 'universe, boundary, system, and series' system, the Mesozoic is a system within the Phanerozoic boundary.",
    "Both systems describe the same time period but use different terminology."
] 

